# hunter_v2 — Python Standards

## Refactoring guide rules

Prefer these standards from `docs/REFACTORING_GUIDE.md`:

### Separation of layers

- `domain/` for business concepts and pure rules
- `application/` or `usecases/` for orchestration
- `infrastructure/` for Django, ORM, and provider coupling
- `presentation/` for views, forms, and template-facing concerns

### Domain purity

For code under `domain/`: no Django imports, pure Python only.

### Naming language

- English for classes, methods, types, value objects, and internal technical names
- Portuguese only for UI strings and established business text shown to users
- Do not rename broad existing surfaces unless explicitly requested

### SOLID and object calisthenics

- One responsibility per class/module
- Prefer guard clauses over nested `else`
- Wrap important primitives when domain value objects already exist
- Use `@dataclass` with `slots=True`, `TypedDict`, and `Protocol` where appropriate

## Code style

### Imports

Group: standard library, third-party, local. One blank line between groups. No wildcard imports.

### Formatting

Ruff is the formatter/linter. Even with `line-length = 320`, wrap for readability.

### Types

Add type hints on all new or changed Python functions. Prefer `str | None`, `list[T]`, `dict[K, V]`. Avoid `Any` except boundary cases.

### Naming

- Classes: `PascalCase`
- Functions, methods, variables: `snake_case`
- Constants: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`
- Tests: descriptive `test_*` names based on behavior

### Django conventions

- Reuse existing forms, services, use cases, and providers
- Use `transaction.atomic()` for multi-step writes
- Use `select_related` / `prefetch_related` to avoid N+1 issues
- Prefer thin views; move orchestration out when it starts growing

### Money, dates, and locale

- Use `Decimal` for money math, never float
- Use timezone-aware helpers from `django.utils.timezone`
- Respect `LANGUAGE_CODE = pt-br` and `TIME_ZONE = America/Sao_Paulo`

### Error handling

Catch specific exceptions. Keep user-facing messages actionable and coherent with PT-BR UX. Do not swallow errors silently.

### Migrations

Use Django migration commands. Avoid editing historical migrations unless explicitly required.

## Testing style

- Default: Django `TestCase` / `SimpleTestCase`
- Keep tests deterministic and isolated
- Prefer behavior assertions over implementation details
- Add regression tests for bug fixes
- Start with the smallest focused test target
- View tests may instantiate CBVs directly and assign `view.request` and `view.workshop` with `RequestFactory`
